---
import ActionLink from "../components/content/ActionLink.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MediaFigure from "../components/content/MediaFigure.astro";
import ContentGrid from "../components/structure/ContentGrid.astro";
import ContentSection from "../components/structure/ContentSection.astro";
import ContactDetails from "../components/content/ContactDetails.astro";
import PageHeading from "../components/structure/PageHeading.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/structure/PageBody.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/structure/SiteHeader.astro";
import galleryImage from "../../images/placeholder-43-landscape.png";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Denver Museum and THG Archives | Connections Museum"
  description="Learn about the Connections Museum and Telecommunications History Group locations in Denver."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <PageBody>
    <PageHeading level="h1">Denver, Colorado</PageHeading>
    <ContentGrid columns={3}>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="arvada" title="Arvada" headingLevel="h2">
          <p><strong>Coming soon!</strong></p>
          <p>
            We currently hope to be able to open our new museum by the end of
            2026, but we're still in the early stages of planning and
            development. Thank you for your patience, and all your support.
          </p>
          <p>
            Please consider
            <ActionLink href="/support.html#donate">making a donation to help support our mission</ActionLink>
            and ensure opening our brand new museum will have a lasting impact,
            and <ActionLink href="/support.html#volunteer">sign up here</ActionLink>
            if you'd like to be one of our early volunteers and help build our new museum!
          </p>
          <p>
            <ActionLink href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RBXu7qJ7dNQ&list=PLm8mbrpAteNnJd5OOXEiONSqwawlf9nqo">
              Watch our announcement YouTube video series!
            </ActionLink>
          </p>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="downtown" title="Downtown" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>
            Connections Museum Denver resides in a place of honor among telecom
            history locations. The 1929 Mountain States Telephone and Telegraph
            Company Headquarters building at 931 14th Street was deemed
            “Colorado’s Bell System Palace.” Finished shortly before the stock
            market crash of 1929, it represented a symbol of the power of
            industry and technology and has long been an important landmark in
            Denver.
          </p>
          <p>
            This museum is located on the prestigious 14th floor of the historic
            building, and shares the floor with the former executive offices from
            the phone company’s past. A private tour of the museum space includes
            a tour through the elegantly furnished executive offices and takes
            advantage of enjoying the impressive architecture and original
            murals created by famed Colorado artist Allen Tupper True.
          </p>
          <p>
            Connections Museum Denver is open for tours. It is located within a
            secure communications facility, so tours are limited and all adult
            tour members must bring a picture ID.
          </p>
          <p>
            Please contact us at
            <ActionLink href="tel:+13032961221">(303) 296-1221</ActionLink> or
            <ActionLink href="mailto:denver@connections.museum">denver@connections.museum</ActionLink>
            to arrange a tour.
          </p>
          <h3>Museum Gallery</h3>
          <!-- TODO(issue): Replace the placeholder graphic with a Denver museum photo and write accurate alternative text. -->
          <p>
            For a taste of what you can see at Connections Museum Denver,
            browse the image below. For a full virtual tour of the old phone
            company building at 931 14th Street, see our full online exhibit.
          </p>
          <MediaFigure image={galleryImage} alt="" />
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="archives" title="Archives" headingLevel="h2">
          <!-- TODO(issue): Review regional capitalization and house style, including “midwest.” -->
          <p>
            Begun in 1990, the THG Archives has grown to become one of the
            nation’s largest industry archives, and includes documentation of
            the telecom industry from its inception in 1876 through the present
            time. It is especially strong in its documentation of companies from
            the midwest and western states.
          </p>
          <!-- TODO(issue): Add the correct online archives URL; the legacy link had no destination. -->
          <p>
            A portion of our collection is available for viewing online.
          </p>
          <h3>What you will find</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Telephone directories</strong> — over 10,000 Bell System and Regional Bell Operating Company phone books from 1890 through the present, representing Arizona, Colorado, Idaho, Iowa, Minnesota, Montana, Nebraska, New Mexico, North Dakota, Oregon, South Dakota, Utah, Washington, and Wyoming.</li>
            <li><strong>Photographs</strong> — over 100,000 prints and 20,000 negatives dating as far back as 1876.</li>
            <li><strong>Historic documents</strong> — records from over 500 historic and several currently operational telephone companies west of the Mississippi, including ledgers, articles of incorporation and dissolution, Board meeting minutes, correspondence, and stock certificates.</li>
            <li><strong>Serials</strong> — union publications, Telephone Pioneer and company newsletters, and magazines from 1900 to the present.</li>
            <li><strong>Memoirs and oral histories</strong> — memoirs, scrapbooks, unpublished manuscripts, and oral histories from telephone company executives and employees.</li>
            <li><strong>Policies and procedures</strong> — regional policies, interdepartmental memos, Bell System Practices, and records retention schedules.</li>
            <li><strong>Audio and video</strong> — advertising, training, and other recordings, including more than 15,000 videotapes, films, and DVDs and nearly 5,000 audiotapes.</li>
            <li><strong>Reference library</strong> — an extensive library of publications about the industry.</li>
            <li><strong>Current events</strong> — we track nearly 3,000 current telephone, wireless, manufacturing, internet, and interactive cable companies around the world through public records such as news clippings and financial reports, as well as industry-related legislation, trends, and statistics.</li>
            <li><strong>Ephemera</strong> — toys, promotional material, service awards, postcards, and other items related to telecommunications.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Research</h3>
          <p>
            The THG Archives is located at 1425 Champa Street in Downtown Denver
            and is open to qualified researchers by appointment. THG staff
            provides research services to customers who are unable to visit in
            person.
          </p>
          <p>
            To schedule an appointment, call
            <ActionLink href="tel:+13032961221">303-296-1221</ActionLink> or
            email <ActionLink href="mailto:denver@connections.museum">denver@connections.museum</ActionLink>.
          </p>
          <h4>By scheduling a visit, you agree to the following policies:</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>No food or drinks are allowed in the research area.</li>
            <li>No pens may be used while performing research. We will provide a pencil if needed.</li>
            <li>To protect documents and photographs, do not wear gloves or use hand sanitizer while handling archive materials. Please wash your hands with soap and water instead.</li>
            <li>A fee of $20 per four-hour session, plus copying charges, applies and is due at the time of your visit.</li>
          </ul>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="donations" title="Donations" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>
            Help support the Telecommunications History Group and its museum
            and archive work through a donation.
          </p>
          <ActionLink href="support.html#donate">Learn about ways to donate</ActionLink>
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
      <r-cell>
        <ContentSection id="contact" title="Contact Us" headingLevel="h2">
          <p>Telecommunications History Group and Connections Museum Denver</p>
          <ContactDetails
            name="THG Archives"
            address="PO Box 8719, Denver, CO 80201-8719"
            phone="+13032961221"
            phoneLabel="303-296-1221"
            email="denver@connections.museum"
          />
        </ContentSection>
      </r-cell>
    </ContentGrid>
  </PageBody>
</BaseLayout>
